        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pure Protein's reputation is budget-friendly, and the bars and shakes often are. The powder is less clear-cut. It is sold mostly in 1 lb and 1.75 lb tubs, and smaller tubs almost always cost more per gram than the 4 to 5 lb tubs many rivals sell. Prices change constantly, so treat the following as approximate ranges from listings we checked.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Approximate cost per 25 g of protein</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.10-1.75</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pure Protein 100% Whey</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.75-2.20</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pure Protein Shake</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.60-2.20</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pure Protein Bar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.00-1.30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Large-tub mainstream whey</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Since one scoop is 25 g of protein, the cost per scoop and the cost per 25 g are the same. A review site that priced it on Amazon found about $1.53 to $1.73 per serving depending on tub size, and grocery and big-box prices are sometimes lower. On a good deal it can dip near $1.10. At full price it is closer to premium whey territory.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with a large tub of a mainstream whey, which commonly works out to roughly $1.00 to $1.30 per 25 g, and Pure Protein rarely wins on value. If you are shopping on Amazon specifically, our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon protein powder</a> guide explains how to compare unit prices and when Subscribe and Save is worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fair takeaway: buy Pure Protein because it is convenient, on sale or the flavor you like, not because you assume it is the cheapest option. Always check the price per ounce on the shelf tag or listing and convert it to price per 25 g.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="powder-vs-shakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Powder vs Shakes vs Bars
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people know Pure Protein from its bars and bottled shakes, so it is worth comparing all three. The shake is the standout. Per the label at the time of writing, an 11 oz bottle gives 30 g of protein for about 140 calories, with under 1 g of sugar and around 6 g of carbohydrate including fiber. That is a leaner ratio than the powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bars are a different product. Each lists about 20 g of protein for roughly 200 calories depending on flavor, sweetened with maltitol and sucralose. Maltitol is a sugar alcohol that can cause gas or loose stools in larger amounts, and buyer reviews mention it. Bars are a snack, not a replacement for a post-workout shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The powder wins on cost and flexibility. You can mix it with milk for more calories or water for fewer, add it to oats or smoothies, and adjust the dose. The shake wins on convenience and protein per calorie. The bar wins only on portability.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are mainly drinking shakes from the fridge, our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink</a> guide compares Pure Protein's bottle with Premier Protein, Fairlife and others. If you are choosing between Pure Protein and Premier Protein powder, our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> lays out the label differences.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sweeteners, Carbs and Additives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 100% Whey ingredient lists we checked include maltodextrin, cocoa or vanilla flavoring, cellulose gum, xanthan gum, lecithin, salt, minerals, acesulfame potassium and sucralose. The lecithin is soy-derived in the labels we saw, and the allergen statement lists milk and soy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acesulfame potassium and sucralose are approved for use in food in the US, and they are why sugar stays at 2 to 3 g per scoop. Some people avoid them on principle or find the aftertaste noticeable. If that is you, Pure Protein is not a fit, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide lists stevia-only and unsweetened options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maltodextrin is the reason for the higher carbohydrate count. It is a quick-digesting starch that adds texture and bulk and is harmless in these amounts for most people. It does make the powder a slightly worse choice for strict low-carb diets, where an isolate with 1 to 2 g of carbohydrate makes more sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gums such as cellulose and xanthan give the shake body. They are common and generally well tolerated, though some people with sensitive stomachs report bloating from gums. If you notice that, try a powder with fewer thickeners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            We could not find a third-party sport certification, such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, on Pure Protein 100% Whey at the time of writing. The brand's marketing focuses on protein content, gluten-free status and BCAAs rather than independent testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most recreational users, that is not a deal-breaker. Whey from a mainstream manufacturer is generally a low-risk product. But it does mean you are relying on the label alone for protein accuracy, and drug-tested athletes should not use it. Competing athletes need a certified product and should check the exact item in the certifier's database.
          </p>

          <p>
            If certification matters at a mainstream price, Muscle Milk Pro Series is NSF Certified for Sport per the brand and often costs less per 25 g than Pure Protein. Our <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk review</a> covers it. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, our alternative pick below, is promoted as Informed Choice certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are a smaller concern for whey than for plant proteins, which tend to carry more from soil. Still, no protein powder is tested by the FDA before sale, so third-party testing is the only independent check. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what certifications cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Pure Protein powder if lowest cost per gram is your goal.</strong> It is fairly priced, not cheap. Large tubs of mainstream whey, including our alternatives below, often deliver 25 g of protein for less.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you are drug-tested.</strong> We found no third-party sport certification. Choose an NSF or Informed Choice product instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you are lactose sensitive or avoid artificial sweeteners.</strong> The concentrate-first blend carries more lactose than an isolate, and every flavor we checked uses acesulfame potassium and sucralose. A whey isolate such as Dymatize ISO100, covered in our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a>, is gentler for most people with dairy issues.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you are cutting hard.</strong> At 160 to 170 calories and 9 to 10 g of carbohydrate per 25 g of protein, it is less efficient than a lean whey or isolate. The Pure Protein Shake, at 30 g for about 140 calories, is a better fit from the same brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Get the Most From It
          </h2>

          <p>
            One scoop is a full 25 g dose, which is the amount most research uses after training. Most adults building muscle do well on roughly 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> shows how a daily shake fits into that total.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix it with water if you are watching calories, or with milk if you want a creamier shake and an extra 8 g of protein per cup. Because of the maltodextrin and gums, it mixes reasonably thick in water, and buyers generally report it dissolves well in a shaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            To keep cost per gram down, buy the largest tub you can find, watch for multi-buy deals at grocery stores, and use Subscribe and Save on Amazon. Compare the unit price against a large tub of a rival every time you restock, because the cheapest option changes often.
          </p>

          <p>
            As with any supplement, a shake adds to meals rather than replacing them. People with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and anyone with a milk or soy allergy or on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first. This review is label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

