          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the budget brand is the cheapest</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pure Protein's bars and shakes have a budget image, but the powder is sold mostly in small tubs that cost more per gram. Divide the price by total grams of protein and multiply by 25. Large tubs of mainstream whey often beat it, so check every time you restock.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing the brand with pure protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Searching pure protein powder returns this brand and also single-ingredient powders. Pure Protein 100% Whey has maltodextrin, gums, flavors and two artificial sweeteners. If you wanted a pure, unflavored protein with one ingredient, you need a different product, such as an unflavored whey isolate or pea isolate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using bars as a shake replacement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Pure Protein bar gives about 20 g of protein for roughly 200 calories, plus maltitol that can upset sensitive stomachs. Eating two or three a day to hit a protein target adds calories and sugar alcohols quickly. Use powder or the bottled shake for daily protein and keep bars for travel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the carbohydrate line on a cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each scoop carries about 9 to 10 g of carbohydrate and 160 to 170 calories for 25 g of protein. That is fine for most people but adds up on a strict diet. If you are cutting, the Pure Protein Shake or a lean isolate gives the same protein for fewer calories.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using it as a drug-tested athlete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                We found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification on Pure Protein powder. Supplements can be contaminated with banned substances, and the athlete carries the risk. Choose a certified powder, such as Muscle Milk Pro Series or Gold Standard, and check the exact product in the certifier's database.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

