          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Pure Protein powder good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a decent mainstream whey. Each scoop gives 25 g of protein from whey concentrate and isolate, and buyers generally like the chocolate and vanilla flavors. The drawbacks are higher carbohydrate than many rivals, two artificial sweeteners, no third-party certification we could find, and a cost per gram that is fair rather than cheap. It is fine for everyday shakes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein is in Pure Protein 100% Whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Per the label at the time of writing, one scoop of about 40 to 41 g gives 25 g of protein. Rich Chocolate lists about 170 calories and vanilla about 160, with 9 to 10 g of carbohydrate and 2 to 3 g of sugar. The protein is a blend of whey protein concentrate and whey protein isolate. Check your tub, as labels vary.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Pure Protein powder cheap?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not especially. At the prices we saw it lands around $1.10 to $1.75 per 25 g of protein, depending on tub size and retailer. That is mid-range. Large tubs of mainstream whey often cost $1.00 to $1.30 per 25 g. The bars and multi-packs of shakes are where Pure Protein is most often discounted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Pure Protein good for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The powder is usable, but at 160 to 170 calories per scoop it is less efficient than leaner wheys. The bottled Pure Protein Shake, at 30 g of protein for about 140 calories with under 1 g of sugar, is the better choice from this brand on a diet. Protein helps with fullness, but total calories still decide results. This is not medical advice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sweeteners are in Pure Protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The 100% Whey labels we checked list acesulfame potassium and sucralose. The bars use maltitol and sucralose. These sweeteners are approved for use in food in the US and keep sugar low. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, look for stevia-sweetened or unflavored powders instead, because none of the Pure Protein products we checked avoid them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Pure Protein third-party tested?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We could not find NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or a similar certification on Pure Protein 100% Whey at the time of writing. That does not mean it is unsafe, but drug-tested athletes should choose a certified product and confirm the exact item in the certifier's database before competing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Pure Protein the same as Premier Protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. They are separate brands from different companies. Premier Protein is owned by BellRing Brands, the same company behind Dymatize. Pure Protein came out of The Bountiful Company's sports nutrition group. Both are best known for ready-to-drink shakes, and both sell a whey powder. Our Premier Protein powder review compares the two labels.
              </p>
